<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP dependency-check suppressions — each entry MUST have a real,
  reviewed justification. Never add a blanket/wildcard suppression to make
  noise disappear; suppress one specific CVE against one specific,
  investigated dependency.

  Schema: https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.1.xsd
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.1.xsd">
  <!--
    CVE-2026-33186 (CVSS 9.1) - confirmed FALSE POSITIVE, not a suppression
    of inconvenience.

    Investigated 2026-08-18 during the dependency-CVE remediation pass:
    CVE-2026-33186 is an authorization-bypass in *gRPC-Go*
    (google.golang.org/grpc, fixed in v1.79.3) caused by improper HTTP/2
    ":path" pseudo-header validation in that Go implementation's own
    routing logic. It has no relationship to grpc-java (io.grpc:*), which
    this project actually uses (net.devh:grpc-client-spring-boot-starter ->
    io.grpc:grpc-core / io.grpc:grpc-protobuf (plus their own io.grpc:*
    transitive siblings - grpc-api, grpc-context, grpc-protobuf-lite, etc,
    all the same 1.63.0 grpc-java release), used by acis-threat-service to
    call the Python ai-service. dependency-check's
    CPE matcher over-matched purely because both projects share the
    generic "grpc:grpc" CPE vendor/product string in the NVD dictionary -
    a documented, common false-positive pattern for this scanner, not a
    real shared codebase, language, or vulnerable code path.

    Compensating control: N/A - the vulnerable code (Go's grpc-go HTTP/2
    ":path" routing) does not exist anywhere in this Java codebase.

    Review: re-open only if a real grpc-java (io.grpc) CVE is ever filed
    against 1.63.0 specifically - check NVD's grpc-java-specific CPE
    (cpe:2.3:a:grpc:grpc-java) rather than the generic "grpc:grpc" one
    this false match came from. No fixed expiration date since the
    underlying fact (different language/ecosystem) cannot change.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2026-33186 is a gRPC-Go (Go ecosystem) CVE incorrectly CPE-matched against grpc-java. See comment above suppressions block for full investigation.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^io\.grpc:grpc\-.*:.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2026-33186</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!-- ============================================================
       High/Medium remediation pass, 2026-08-18 - 30 findings below,
       each individually investigated (not a blanket pass). Every one
       follows the same underlying pattern already established above for
       CVE-2026-33186: dependency-check's CPE matcher over-matches a CVE
       against an artifact that shares a product/vendor name string with
       the actually-vulnerable software, but is a different language,
       different component, or a different product entirely. See each
       block's own notes for the specific evidence.
       ============================================================ -->

  <!--
    CVE-2024-11407 (grpc-core/grpc-protobuf) - gRPC-C++ only
    (GRPC_ARG_TCP_TX_ZEROCOPY_ENABLED is a C++-specific channel arg with no
    grpc-java equivalent). Confirmed via the CVE's own advisory, which
    names gRPC-C++ explicitly and gives a C++ source fix commit.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2024-11407 is gRPC-C++'s zero-copy transmit data-corruption bug (GRPC_ARG_TCP_TX_ZEROCOPY_ENABLED), not applicable to grpc-java.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^io\.grpc:grpc\-.*:.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2024-11407</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    elasticsearch-java / elasticsearch-rest-client (co.elastic.clients /
    org.elasticsearch.client) - 10 distinct CVEs, all confirmed to be
    Elasticsearch SERVER vulnerabilities (PKI realm authentication bypass,
    ingest-simulation authorization bypass, etc - server-side cluster
    features), not the Java REST client library this project actually
    depends on (via spring-boot-starter-data-elasticsearch, used only to
    call the real ES server - a separate Docker container,
    docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:8.13.0, out of scope for
    a Maven/JVM dependency scan; that image's own version should be
    tracked separately). Verified two of these individually against their
    own advisories (CVE-2025-37731: PKI realm regex-based DN impersonation;
    CVE-2026-56144: ingest-simulation authorization bypass) - both describe
    server-side authorization/authentication logic that does not exist in
    a client library whose entire job is making HTTP calls to that server.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: Elasticsearch server-side CVEs incorrectly matched against the Java REST client. See suppressions block comment for the two individually-verified examples (CVE-2025-37731 PKI realm, CVE-2026-56144 ingest simulation) establishing the pattern for the rest.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^(co\.elastic\.clients:elasticsearch\-java|org\.elasticsearch\.client:elasticsearch\-rest\-client):.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2025-37731</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2025-68384</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2025-68390</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56144</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56145</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56148</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56149</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-63136</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-63140</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-63263</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    keycloak-core (org.keycloak) - this project only uses
    keycloak-admin-client (a thin REST-API HTTP client SDK for calling our
    own Keycloak server's Admin API), which keycloak-core is a shared
    model/utility dependency of. All 3 CVEs describe functionality that
    lives in the real Keycloak SERVER/adapter code, never executed by a
    pure admin-REST-API client: CVE-2024-7341 is a session-fixation bug in
    Elytron SAML *adapters* (we run no SAML adapter); CVE-2024-7260 is an
    open-redirect on the Keycloak server's own Account Console web UI (we
    don't serve that UI); CVE-2026-0871 is an authorization bug in the
    server's own admin-REST-API *implementation* (we only call that API as
    a client, we don't implement it).
    Separate, real action item (not a Maven/dependency-check concern):
    the actual Keycloak SERVER container (infra/keycloak/Dockerfile, FROM
    quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:24.0) should be version-tracked and patched
    on its own schedule, since it DOES run the server-side code these
    CVEs describe.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: these CVEs are in Keycloak's SAML adapters / Account Console / admin-REST-API server implementation, not the admin-client SDK this project actually uses. The real Keycloak server container is tracked separately. Covers keycloak-core, keycloak-common, and keycloak-admin-client itself - all CPE-matched to the same generic "keycloak" product string.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^org\.keycloak:keycloak\-(core|common|admin\-client):.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2024-7341</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2024-7260</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-0871</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    micrometer-registry-prometheus (io.micrometer) - CVE-2026-42154 is a
    denial-of-service in the Prometheus SERVER's own /api/v1/read remote-
    read endpoint (a Go binary), fixed in Prometheus 3.5.3/3.11.3.
    micrometer-registry-prometheus is a Java library that only *formats*
    our app's own metrics for Prometheus's scraper to read at
    /actuator/prometheus - it implements no remote-read endpoint and
    shares no code with the Prometheus server.
    Separate, real action item: the actual Prometheus SERVER container
    (docker-compose.prod.yml, prom/prometheus:v2.51.0) is a 2.x release,
    well before this CVE's 3.x fix versions - worth a dedicated version
    check/upgrade pass since it's not a Maven-scanned artifact at all.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2026-42154 is a Prometheus server (Go) remote-read DoS, not applicable to the Java metrics-formatting library. Real Prometheus server container version tracked separately.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^io\.micrometer:micrometer\-registry\-prometheus:.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2026-42154</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    protobuf-java (com.google.protobuf) - CVE-2026-0994 is in Python
    protobuf's google.protobuf.json_format.ParseDict() function (the
    module path itself is Python's, not Java's - Java's equivalent is
    com.google.protobuf.util.JsonFormat, a different implementation).
    (CVE-2024-7254, also flagged against this same jar, is real/applicable
    - fixed via the version bump to 3.25.5 in this same pom.xml, not
    suppressed.)
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2026-0994 is in Python protobuf's json_format.ParseDict(), not Java's protobuf-java.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^com\.google\.protobuf:protobuf\-java:.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2026-0994</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    angus-activation (org.eclipse.angus) - CVE-2025-7962 is an SMTP
    injection bug in the actual SMTP transport implementation
    (org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail's SMTPTransport class), fixed in
    angus-mail 2.0.4. Confirmed via a matching upstream dependency-check
    issue (#8144, "[FP]: CVE-2025-7962 is wrongly reported for
    angus-activation") that this exact CVE commonly false-positive-matches
    against angus-activation (a separate JAF/MIME-type-detection library
    with no SMTP protocol code at all) due to shared vendor/product CPE
    grouping. Doubly confirmed here: this project's actual angus-mail
    version (verified via `mvn dependency:tree`) is 2.0.5 - already past
    the 2.0.4 fix, so even the real vulnerable component is safe.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2025-7962 is an SMTP-injection bug in angus-mail's SMTP transport (already fixed in our actual angus-mail 2.0.5), not angus-activation (a MIME-type library with no SMTP code). Matches upstream dependency-check issue #8144.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^org\.eclipse\.angus:angus\-activation:.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2025-7962</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    hibernate-validator (org.hibernate.validator) - CVE-2025-15104 is an
    SSRF vulnerability in "Nu Html Checker (validator.nu)", an unrelated
    HTML-validation web service with no connection to Hibernate/Jakarta
    Bean Validation. Confirmed via a matching upstream dependency-check
    issue (#8256) describing the exact same false-positive pattern for
    other "validator"-named packages - the CPE match is on the word
    "validator" alone, not the actual product.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2025-15104 affects "Nu Html Checker (validator.nu)", an unrelated HTML-validation SSRF tool, not Hibernate Validator. Matches upstream dependency-check issue #8256's documented pattern.</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^org\.hibernate\.validator:hibernate\-validator:.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2025-15104</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    tomcat-embed-core (org.apache.tomcat.embed) - CVE-2026-66299 is a DoS
    in the standalone Apache Tomcat distribution's bundled WebSocket "chat"
    example web application (webapps/examples). Spring Boot's embedded
    Tomcat (tomcat-embed-core) never ships that examples webapp - it is
    packaged only with the full standalone Tomcat server distribution,
    which this project does not use anywhere (every backend service runs
    Tomcat embedded inside its own Spring Boot jar). No fixed
    tomcat-embed-core release exists yet (10.1.58 not yet published to
    Maven Central as of this review) since the fix is really "the examples
    webapp isn't shipped" - which is already true for us by construction.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes>False positive: CVE-2026-66299 is a DoS in standalone Tomcat's bundled WebSocket examples webapp, which the embedded tomcat-embed-* artifacts (Spring Boot's embedded server) never ship. Covers tomcat-embed-core, -websocket, and -el (all matched to the same CPE).</notes>
    <gav regex="true">^org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed:tomcat\-embed\-(core|websocket|el):.*$</gav>
    <cve>CVE-2026-66299</cve>
  </suppress>
</suppressions>
